{ There are three different rosette types, with two reflections for each type, for a total of six different rosettes.
1. The defender does well to create a rosette at any opportunity.
2. The more space the black pieces have, the less the attacker wants to create a rosette. The less space the black pieces have, the more the attacker wants to create a rosette. If the black king is on the edge, sometimes creating a rosette is the only way to prevail.
3. The defense always follows the same strategy: move pieces towards the center of the board, or towards the center of the edge if in a corner. Sometimes the defender can accomplish this by moving his king. More often the rook rejoins the king while attacking the opposing pieces. In other locations on the board, the rook may fall back one or two squares to interrupt the queen's natural attacking lines and allow the king to slip behind the rook.
4. The attacker tries to drive the defending pieces together and push the king towards the edge of the board. Often this is done with an initial check, but if white has less space than black, or is against the edge of the board, it may be more effective to encircle black's pieces, as with Qg3+ in this position. }
1... Rf4+ *


